 "assumptions": [
  "Generation work per night is read from the addendum-9 seed-1 result (nights[i].tagging.turns, names_pass.pairs, update_detection.candidates, dream_stats.items / now_gen / qa_gen / dropped_bad_answer, with_dreams, rem_stats[c].chain_gen / gist_gen / kept / dropped_unverified / dropped_dup, steps); the same arm on the big base would see somewhat different counts.",
  "Not stored, so assumed: 'now' prompts = ceil(now_gen / 2); dream verification prompts = qa_gen + now_gen - dropped_bad_answer (upper bound); probes = with_dreams; ARC validation = 100 questions per night; prompt lengths per phase = PROMPT_TOK (only prefill depends on them).",
  "Every generate batch decodes max_new steps (an upper bound: a batch stops when all rows end). 'typical_0.6' cuts the long generations (tagging 160, dreams 400 x scale, REM 40) to 60% of max_new; this fraction is a guess, not a measurement. Dream decode steps are measured over a few dozen steps and assumed constant out to 400 x scale (dream_long checks the slope).",
  "Decode seconds per step are measured at the batch sizes in inputs (adapter off); a batch smaller than the smallest measured costs as that one, in between log2-interpolated. Training uses the measured seconds per Trainer step at the tok budget for every step (the real batches are longer or shorter than the synthetic one: the open-book examples carry the diary notes).",
  "Not counted: the download, CPU-side bookkeeping (BM25 search, parsing), SHY, the adapter snapshot / restore, the ARC-Easy download. The experts implementation that was fastest per phase in the bench is assumed for both phases (see experts.note in the bench JSON)."
 ],
